WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.600 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A park designer wants to copy a triangular flowerbed farther
along a path without turning or resizing it.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.480 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How can every corner arrive in the right place?

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:16.492 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we will investigate translations: movements that slide
a whole shape while keeping its size, shape and orientation.

00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:16.932 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to translate a shape using one shared
movement.

00:00:16.932 --> 00:00:28.012 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will move from objects or hands-on actions to drawings,
then connect those drawings with precise mathematical
descriptions.

00:00:28.012 --> 00:00:29.372 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep paper nearby if you would like to test an idea for
yourself.

00:00:29.372 --> 00:00:34.966 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can pause the player whenever you need more time.

00:00:34.966 --> 00:00:39.126 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to translate a shape using one shared
movement.

00:00:39.126 --> 00:00:41.926 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to explain why a result is correct, as well as
obtain the result.

00:00:41.926 --> 00:00:49.357 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you watch, look for the evidence that would help you
convince a partner who has not seen your working.

00:00:49.357 --> 00:00:52.037 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here are our three success criteria.

00:00:52.037 --> 00:00:54.637 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can describe direction and distance.

00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:56.997 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can move every vertex equally.

00:00:56.997 --> 00:00:59.197 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can explain what stays the same.

00:00:59.197 --> 00:01:03.037 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check these ideas in two worked examples,
investigate a common mistake and finish with an independent
challenge.

00:01:03.037 --> 00:01:11.773 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good explanation links what you see in the model with the
mathematical rule being used.

00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:15.693 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Cut or trace a triangle on paper and place it on a grid.

00:01:15.693 --> 00:01:17.453 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Mark its starting corners.

00:01:17.453 --> 00:01:21.893 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Slide the triangle gently to a new place without rotating
the paper.

00:01:21.893 --> 00:01:25.693 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare each original corner with its matching new corner.

00:01:25.693 --> 00:01:29.572 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
They should all have travelled the same distance in the same
direction.

00:01:29.572 --> 00:01:33.652 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Saying slide it over there is not enough to reproduce a
translation.

00:01:33.652 --> 00:01:35.612 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Give a direction and distance for the horizontal move, then
for any vertical move.

00:01:35.612 --> 00:01:45.042 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The instruction three right and two up tells a partner
exactly how to move every point on our unit grid.

00:01:45.042 --> 00:01:48.722 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Label the original triangle corners A, B and C.

00:01:48.722 --> 00:01:52.642 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their images are the matching corners after the translation.

00:01:52.642 --> 00:01:59.722 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the labels paired so that A is compared with its own
image, rather than whichever new corner happens to be
closest.

00:01:59.722 --> 00:02:03.241 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Matching points give reliable evidence about the movement.

00:02:03.241 --> 00:02:05.561 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Vertex A starts at one, one.

00:02:05.561 --> 00:02:09.081 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Translate the shape three units right and two units up.

00:02:09.081 --> 00:02:11.361 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the new coordinates of A.

00:02:11.361 --> 00:02:14.921 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about which coordinate changes at each stage.

00:02:14.921 --> 00:02:19.668 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the other corners hidden for a moment so one checked
point leads the method.

00:02:24.668 --> 00:02:27.548 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three right changes one, one to four, one.

00:02:27.548 --> 00:02:29.308 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two up then gives four, three.

00:02:29.308 --> 00:02:31.468 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The image of A is four, three.

00:02:31.468 --> 00:02:35.548 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This coordinate calculation records the slide shown on the
grid.

00:02:35.548 --> 00:02:41.178 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It does not rotate the point around the origin or exchange
the two coordinate positions.

00:02:41.258 --> 00:02:44.298 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
B begins at three, one and moves to six, three.

00:02:44.298 --> 00:02:47.618 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
C begins at one, three and moves to four, five.

00:02:47.618 --> 00:02:52.898 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each first coordinate increased by three, and each second
coordinate increased by two.

00:02:52.898 --> 00:02:57.905 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Join the image vertices in the same order to obtain the
translated triangle.

00:02:57.905 --> 00:03:02.625 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If A moves three right but B and C stay still, the triangle
changes shape.

00:03:02.625 --> 00:03:05.945 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That is not a translation of the whole triangle.

00:03:05.945 --> 00:03:10.145 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Repair the model by applying the same instruction to every
vertex.

00:03:10.145 --> 00:03:15.217 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then compare the side lengths and corner angles with those
of the original triangle.

00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:19.737 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An invariant is something that stays the same during a
transformation.

00:03:19.737 --> 00:03:24.177 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In a translation, side lengths, angles and orientation stay
the same.

00:03:24.177 --> 00:03:25.617 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Position changes.

00:03:25.617 --> 00:03:32.768 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The triangle can point the same way in a new location, so
pointing direction alone does not tell us where it has
moved.

00:03:32.768 --> 00:03:37.288 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now start again with A at one, one, B at three, one and C at
one, three.

00:03:37.288 --> 00:03:41.128 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Slide every vertex one unit left and two units up.

00:03:41.128 --> 00:03:43.208 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the image of B.

00:03:43.208 --> 00:03:47.725 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check that the new shape will stay within the displayed
first-quadrant grid.

00:03:52.805 --> 00:03:55.245 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
B moves from three, one to two, three.

00:03:55.245 --> 00:03:58.645 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A moves to zero, three, and C moves to zero, five.

00:03:58.645 --> 00:04:03.005 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decreasing each first coordinate by one shifts the triangle
left.

00:04:03.005 --> 00:04:06.445 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Increasing each second coordinate by two shifts it up.

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:09.746 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The axis points are valid positions on our grid.

00:04:09.746 --> 00:04:15.346 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To return the second image to its starting position, slide
one unit right and two units down.

00:04:15.346 --> 00:04:18.866 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These reverse movements undo the original changes.

00:04:18.866 --> 00:04:22.546 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check one pair of coordinates, then check all vertices.

00:04:22.546 --> 00:04:27.637 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A successful reverse operation provides another way to test
a translation.

00:04:27.637 --> 00:04:33.477 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two triangles may be the same size and shape without being
related by the translation you were given.

00:04:33.477 --> 00:04:36.037 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One might have been turned or reflected.

00:04:36.037 --> 00:04:44.396 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To identify this particular slide, compare corresponding
vertices and verify the same horizontal and vertical changes
for every pair.

00:04:44.396 --> 00:04:50.556 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A repeated motif can be created by translating a single
shape by the same step several times.

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.676 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep a record of each step so the copies line up
predictably.

00:04:52.676 --> 00:04:59.733 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The step controls the spacing; it does not change the size
of the original motif.

00:04:59.733 --> 00:05:02.693 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A student claims two points were translated by the same
movement.

00:05:02.693 --> 00:05:03.733 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first moved three right and two up.

00:05:03.733 --> 00:05:04.053 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The second moved three right and one up.

00:05:04.053 --> 00:05:12.173 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can these both belong to one translated shape?

00:05:12.173 --> 00:05:15.769 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decide which part of the shared movement rule has failed.

00:05:20.769 --> 00:05:25.809 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertical changes differ, so the two point movements do
not follow one translation.

00:05:25.809 --> 00:05:30.209 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every point must move by the same horizontal and vertical
amounts.

00:05:30.209 --> 00:05:34.289 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Correct the second movement to two up, then check any
remaining vertices.

00:05:34.289 --> 00:05:38.648 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Equal changes are stronger evidence than a rough visual
resemblance.

00:05:38.648 --> 00:05:40.408 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal.

00:05:40.408 --> 00:05:43.528 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Translate a shape using one shared movement.

00:05:43.528 --> 00:05:48.488 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one example from today and explain the evidence that
made its answer convincing.

00:05:48.488 --> 00:05:53.128 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which of our three success criteria feels secure, and which
needs another try?

00:05:53.128 --> 00:05:59.686 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Revisit a model, test your explanation with a partner, and
describe one mistake you can now notice and correct.
